  "text": "Solution 2 is the most likely correct answer, though I can't verify it from memory.\n\n- **Solution 1 (5)** rests on a faulty claim. Quasi-hyperelliptic surfaces in characteristics 2 and 3 have $q = 0$, and hyperelliptic surfaces have $q = 1$, so none of them has $h^1(\\mathcal{O}_X) = 2$. Its counterexamples therefore don't exist, and its claim that characteristic 3 fails is unsupported.\n- **Solution 3 (2)** asserts a Bombieri–Mumford classification valid for all primes. That is contradicted by the non-classical behaviour in characteristic 2, so it can't be right.\n- **Solution 2 (3)** says characteristic 2 has counterexamples and that the criterion holds for $p \\ge 3$. This matches the pattern I'd expect, since characteristic 2 is where Bombieri–Mumford's non-classical surfaces are most irregular. I'm not certain of the citation to Katsura or of the exact threshold, so this is a moderate-confidence judgment rather than a checked result.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
